{{smaller block|And shewyd to me all the Castyll with in The towers, the wallys are sore ''brosyd'' and brokyn with the erthe qwake which was in Aprill last past.
{{right|''Torkington'', Diarie of Eng. Travell, p. 18.}}
He rode ouer hym on horsebak thre or foure tymes, and ''broused'' hym sore and foule that nygh he was therwith slayn.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 476.}}}}
'''2.''' To crush by beating or pounding; pound; bray, as drugs or articles of food.
{{smaller block|Man, like to cassia, is prov’d best, being ''bruis’d''.
{{right|''Webster'', Duchess of Malfi, iii. 5.}}}}
'''3.''' Figuratively, to beat down or oppress; cudgel, as the brain; scourge; damage.
{{smaller block|''Bruis’d'' underneath the yoke of tyranny.
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., v. 2.}}
I will ''bruise'' my brains and confine myself to much vexation.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Woman-Hater, v. 2.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To fight with the fists; box.
{{smaller block|''Bruising'' was considered a fine, manly old English custom.
{{right|''Thackeray.''}}}}

'''bruise''' (bröz), ''n.'' [< ''bruise'', ''v.''] A contusion; a superficial injury caused by impact, without laceration, as of an animal body, a plant, or other impressible object.

'''bruiser''' (brö′zėr), ''n.'' '''1.''' One who bruises.—'''2.''' A concave tool for grinding the specula of telescopes. {{smaller|It is made of brass, about a quarter of an inch thick, hammered as near the gage as possible. By this instrument the speculum is prepared for the hands of the polisher.}}
'''3.''' The name of various machines for bruising grain, etc., for feeding cattle.—'''4.''' A boxer; a pugilist; a bully.
{{smaller block|<poem>For do not men delight—
We call them men — our ''bruisers'' to excite,
And urge with bribing gold, and feed them for the fight?</poem>}}

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Crabbe.''}}
Gentlemen were ''bruisers'', and ''bruisers'' were gentlemen.
{{right|''J. Hawthorne'', Dust, p. 7.}}}}
'''5.''' A name applied to various plants supposed to be efficacious in healing bruises, as bruisewort, soapwort, etc. [Eng.]

¶
'''bruisewort''' (bröz′wėrt), ''n.'' [ME. ''brysewort'', < ''brysen'', bruise, + ''wort'', wort.] A name given to several plants, as the daisy (''Bellis perennis''), the soapwort (''Saponaria officinalis''), etc., from their supposed efficacy in healing bruises.
{{smaller block|In the curious treatise of the virtues of herbs, Royal MS. 18 A. vi., fol. 72 b, is mentioned “''brysewort'', or bonwort, or daysye, consolida minor, good to breke bocches.”
{{right|''Way'', Promptorium, p. 52, note.}}}}

'''bruising''' (brö′zing), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''bruise'', ''v.''] '''1.''' In ''flax-working'', the process of passing flax, after retting, between grooved rollers, to break the woody portion; scutching.—'''2.''' A method of treating hides by rubbing the grained side with a graining-board.—'''3.''' In ''wine-making'', the process of pounding or stamping grapes with a wooden maul or pestle, to soften the skins and fleshy part.

'''bruit''' (bröt), ''n.'' [< ME. ''brut'', ''bruyt'', ''brout'', < OF. ''bruit'', ''brui'', F. ''bruit'', noise, uproar, rumor (= Pr. ''bruich'', ''bruit'', ''brut'' = It. ''bruito''; ML. ''brugitus''), < OF. ''bruire'', F. ''bruire'' = Pr. ''brugir'', ''bruzir'' = It. ''bruire'', rustle, roar; of uncertain origin.] '''1.''' Report; rumor; fame.
{{smaller block|A ''bruit'' ran from one to the other that the king was slain.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney.''}}
There came an uncertaine ''bruite'' from Barbados of some disorder there.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, June 26, 1671.}}
To view what ''bruit'' by virtue got, their lives could justly}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|crave.
{{right|''A Praise of Mistress Ryce'', Arber’s Eng. Garner, I. 38.}}}}
'''2.''' A noise; a loud sound; a din.
{{smaller block|<poem>Some fresh ''bruit''
Startled me all aheap.</poem>
{{right|''Hood.''}}}}
'''3.''' [Mod. F., pron. brwē.] In ''pathol.'', the name given to sounds of various nature, in general abnormal, produced in the body, or evoked in it, by percussion or succussion: used to some extent in English.—{{smaller|'''Bruit de galop,''' a cardiac sound suggesting a gallop, the normal first sound being preceded by a faint presystolic sound.—'''Bruit de scie,''' a rough cardiac murmur, suggesting the sound of a saw.—'''Bruit du diable''' (devil’s bruit), a continuous humming sound heard in the jugular veins at the base of the neck; venous hum. It is more frequent and more marked in young persons than in adults, and in anemic than in normal states.}}

'''bruit''' (bröt), ''v.'' [< ''bruit'', ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To announce with noise; report; noise abroad.
{{smaller block|<poem>By this great clatter one of the greatest note
Seems ''bruited.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, v. 7.}}
<poem>Thou art no less than fame hath ''bruited.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., ii. 3.}}
It is marvell to think what his friends meant, to let come abroad such shallow reasonings with the name of a man so much ''bruited'' for learning.
{{right|''Milton'', Church-Government, i. 5.}}
<poem>But a dark rumour will be ''bruited'' up,
From tribe to tribe, until it reach his ear.</poem>
{{right|''M. Arnold'', Sohrab and Rustum.}}}}
'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To give forth sound; sound.
{{smaller block|<poem>Bronze clarions awake and faintly ''bruit.''</poem>
{{right|''Keats'', Endymion, i.}}}}

